    "description": "The internal WiFi module on the Compute Module 4 (that's the bit under the metal shield in the picture above) routes its antenna signal via software. You can route the signal to either:\n\nThe built-in PCB triangle antenna (this is the default).\nThe external U.FL connector (which has an external antenna plugged into it in the picture above)\n\nTo switch the signal to the U.FL connector (for example, if you're installing your CM4 in a metal box where the PCB antenna would be useless), you need to edit the boot config file (sudo nano /boot/firmware/config.txt, and add the following at the bottom:",
    "external": {},
    "html": "<html><head lang=\"en\"><meta charset=\"utf-8\"><meta http-equiv=\"X-UA-Compatible\" content=\"IE=edge\"><title>Enable the external antenna connector on the Raspberry Pi Compute Module 4/5 - Jeff Geerling</title><meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width,initial-scale=1\"><meta name=\"description\" content=\"\n\n\nThe internal WiFi module on the Compute Module 4 (that's the bit under the metal shield in the picture above) routes its antenna signal via software. You can route the signal to either:\n\nThe built-in PCB triangle antenna (this is the default).\nThe external U.FL connector (which has an external antenna plugged into it in the picture above)\n\nTo switch the signal to the U.FL connector (for example, if you're installing your CM4 in a metal box where the PCB antenna would be useless), you need to edit the boot config file (sudo nano /boot/firmware/config.txt, and add the following at the bottom:\"><meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"\"><meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://www.jeffgeerling.com/blog/2022/enable-external-antenna-connector-on-raspberry-pi-compute-module-45/\"><meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"Jeff Geerling\"><meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Enable the external antenna connector on the Raspberry Pi Compute Module 4/5\"><meta property=\"og:description\" content=\"The internal WiFi module on the Compute Module 4 (that's the bit under the metal shield in the picture above) routes its antenna signal via software. You can route the signal to either:\nThe built-in PCB triangle antenna (this is the default). The external U.FL connector (which has an external antenna plugged into it in the picture above) To switch the signal to the U.FL connector (for example, if you're installing your CM4 in a metal box where the PCB antenna would be useless), you need to edit the boot config file (sudo nano /boot/firmware/config.txt, and add the following at the bottom:\"><meta property=\"og:locale\" content=\"en_us\"><meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\"><meta property=\"article:section\" content=\"blog\"><meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2022-02-04T22:31:10+00:00\"><meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2022-02-04T22:31:10+00:00\"><meta property=\"article:tag\" content=\"Antenna\"><meta property=\"article:tag\" content=\"Cm4\"><meta property=\"article:tag\" content=\"Compute Module\"><meta property=\"article:tag\" content=\"Config\"><meta property=\"article:tag\" content=\"Raspberry Pi\"><meta property=\"article:tag\" content=\"Wifi\"><meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary\"><meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Enable the external antenna connector on the Raspberry Pi Compute Module 4/5\"><meta name=\"twitter:description\" content=\"The internal WiFi module on the Compute Module 4 (that's the bit under the metal shield in the picture above) routes its antenna signal via software. You can route the signal to either:\nThe built-in PCB triangle antenna (this is the default). The external U.FL connector (which has an external antenna plugged into it in the picture above) To switch the signal to the U.FL connector (for example, if you're installing your CM4 in a metal box where the PCB antenna would be useless), you need to edit the boot config file (sudo nano /boot/firmware/config.txt, and add the following at the bottom:\"><link href=\"https://www.jeffgeerling.com/css/fonts.2c2227b81b1970a03e760aa2e6121cd01f87c88586803cbb282aa224720a765f.css\" rel=\"stylesheet\"><link rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" media=\"screen\" href=\"https://www.jeffgeerling.com/css/main.7a5c05040394322612a2d98397e9932aa81d9d7acc70199463e79f8180c8e0c0.css\"><link id=\"darkModeStyle\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" href=\"https://www.jeffgeerling.com/css/dark.478e7f2b9cf8d87be42edfd9fe1810beab1331847df94b1b430478b309f28282.css\" media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\"><link rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" href=\"https://www.jeffgeerling.com/css/custom.ae3675b509468575414dc5607603a59823aaf6ca49d50aeb33d52ebc1964f61c.css\"><script async=\"\" src=\"//analytics.midwesternmac.com/tracker.js\" id=\"fathom-script\"></script><script type=\"module\" defer=\"\" src=\"https://comments.jeffgeerling.com/web/embed.mjs\"></script></head><body><div class=\"content\"><header><div class=\"main\"><a href=\"https://www.jeffgeerling.com/\">Jeff Geerling</a></div><nav><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/c/JeffGeerling\">YouTube</a>\n<a href=\"https://www.redshirtjeff.com/\">Merch</a>\n<a href=\"/\">Blog</a>\n<a href=\"/about/\">About</a>\n<a href=\"/blog.xml\">RSS</a></nav></header><main><article><div class=\"post-container\"><div class=\"post-content\"><div class=\"title\"><h1 class=\"title\">Enable the external antenna connector on the Raspberry Pi Compute Module 4/5</h1><div class=\"meta\">Feb 4, 2022</div></div><section class=\"body\"><figure class=\"insert-image\"><img src=\"/blog/2022/enable-external-antenna-connector-on-raspberry-pi-compute-module-45/external-antenna-raspberry-pi-compute-module-4.jpeg\" alt=\"Raspberry Pi Compute Module 4 external U.FL antenna\" width=\"700\" height=\"466\"></figure><p>The internal WiFi module on the Compute Module 4 (that's the bit under the metal shield in the picture above) routes its antenna signal via software. You can route the signal to either:</p><ol><li>The built-in PCB triangle antenna (this is the default).</li><li>The external U.FL connector (which has an external antenna plugged into it in the picture above)</li></ol><p>To switch the signal to the U.FL connector (for example, if you're installing your CM4 in a metal box where the PCB antenna would be useless), you need to edit the boot config file (<code>sudo nano /boot/firmware/config.txt</code>, and add the following at the bottom:</p><div class=\"highlight\"><pre tabindex=\"0\" style=\"color:#e6edf3;background-color:#0d1117;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none\"><code class=\"language-fallback\" data-lang=\"fallback\"><span style=\"display:flex\"><span># Switch to external antenna.\n</span></span><span style=\"display:flex\"><span>dtparam=ant2\n</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Then reboot the Pi.</p><p>To switch back, comment out or delete the <code>dtparam</code> line above. Note that you can actually disable the WiFi antenna route <em>entirely</em> (even without disabling the WiFi chip); here are the docs <a href=\"https://github.com/raspberrypi/firmware/blob/master/boot/overlays/README\">straight from the firmware repository</a>:</p><div class=\"highlight\"><pre tabindex=\"0\" style=\"color:#e6edf3;background-color:#0d1117;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none\"><code class=\"language-fallback\" data-lang=\"fallback\"><span style=\"display:flex\"><span>Params:\n</span></span><span style=\"display:flex\"><span>        ant1                    Select antenna 1 (default). CM4/5 only.\n</span></span><span style=\"display:flex\"><span>\n</span></span><span style=\"display:flex\"><span>        ant2                    Select antenna 2. CM4/5 only.\n</span></span><span style=\"display:flex\"><span>\n</span></span><span style=\"display:flex\"><span>        noant                   Disable both antennas. CM4/5 only.\n</span></span></code></pre></div><p>See my <a href=\"/blog/2020/raspberry-pi-compute-module-4-review\">original review of the Compute Module 4</a> for more information about antenna performance, and a recommendation of an external antenna to buy (though you can't go wrong with the one from Raspberry Pi themselves!).</p></section><div class=\"related-posts\"><h2 class=\"title\">Further reading:</h2><ul class=\"list\"><li class=\"mb2\"><a href=\"/blog/2022/blikvm-pcie-puts-computer-your-computer/\">BliKVM PCIe puts a computer in your computer</a></li><li class=\"mb2\"><a href=\"/blog/2022/ptp-and-ieee-1588-hardware-timestamping-on-raspberry-pi-cm4/\">PTP and IEEE-1588 hardware timestamping on the Raspberry Pi CM4</a></li><li class=\"mb2\"><a href=\"/blog/2022/industrial-raspberry-pi-computers-one-explosion-proof/\">Industrial Raspberry Pi computers (one is explosion-proof)</a></li></ul></div><div class=\"post-tags\"><nav class=\"nav tags\"><ul class=\"tags\"><li><a href=\"/tags/antenna/\">antenna</a></li><li><a href=\"/tags/cm4/\">cm4</a></li><li><a href=\"/tags/compute-module/\">compute module</a></li><li><a href=\"/tags/config/\">config</a></li><li><a href=\"/tags/raspberry-pi/\">raspberry pi</a></li><li><a href=\"/tags/wifi/\">wifi</a></li></ul></nav></div></div></div><div class=\"comments\"><h2 class=\"title\">Comments</h2><script>var remark_config={host:\"https://comments.jeffgeerling.com\",site_id:\"jeffgeerling_com\",theme:window.matchMedia&&window.matchMedia(\"(prefers-color-scheme:dark)\").matches?\"dark\":\"light\",simple_view:!0,show_email_subscription:!1,show_rss_subscription:!1,no_footer:!0};window.matchMedia(\"(prefers-color-scheme:dark)\").addEventListener(\"change\",e=>{const t=e.matches?\"dark\":\"light\";window.REMARK42.changeTheme(t)})</script><script>!function(e,t){for(s=0;s<e.length;s++){var s,n=t.createElement(\"script\"),o=\".js\",i=t.head||t.body;\"noModule\"in n?(n.type=\"module\",o=\".mjs\"):n.async=!0,n.defer=!0,n.src=remark_config.host+\"/web/\"+e[s]+o,i.appendChild(n)}}(remark_config.components||[\"embed\"],document)</script><div id=\"remark42\"><iframe src=\"https://comments.jeffgeerling.com/web/iframe.html?host=https%3A%2F%2Fcomments.jeffgeerling.com&amp;site_id=jeffgeerling_com&amp;theme=light&amp;simple_view=true&amp;show_email_subscription=false&amp;show_rss_subscription=false&amp;no_footer=true&amp;url=https%3A%2F%2Fjeffgeerling.com%2Fblog%2F2022%2Fenable-external-antenna-connector-on-raspberry-pi-compute-module-45\" name=\"{}\" frameborder=\"0\" allowtransparency=\"true\" scrolling=\"no\" tabindex=\"0\" title=\"Comments | Remark42\" horizontalscrolling=\"no\" verticalscrolling=\"no\" style=\"height: 100%; width: 100%; border-width: medium; borde